WEBVTT

00:00:01.700 --> 00:00:04.358
Meet MXI RoboICL.

00:00:04.638 --> 00:00:08.137
Our team builds on learning through context, with geometry

00:00:08.137 --> 00:00:10.770
that helps robots act more precisely.

00:00:12.098 --> 00:00:13.917
Start with one demonstration.

00:00:14.210 --> 00:00:17.810
Short windows, sampled across time, show the model how the

00:00:17.810 --> 00:00:19.218
task unfolds.

00:00:19.510 --> 00:00:23.230
Each window connects what the robot sees, the actions taken,

00:00:23.522 --> 00:00:24.718
and what happens next.

00:00:25.010 --> 00:00:27.680
The current episode follows the same format.

00:00:27.960 --> 00:00:31.572
Selected earlier interactions and the latest result join the

00:00:31.572 --> 00:00:33.817
demonstration in the model’s context.

00:00:34.110 --> 00:00:37.292
The model decides, acts, and observes again.

00:00:37.585 --> 00:00:38.868
Its weights stay frozen.

00:00:39.160 --> 00:00:41.442
Experience changes the conversation.

00:00:45.740 --> 00:00:49.155
Here are two wrist images from Fill Pen Holder. In the

00:00:49.155 --> 00:00:52.824
demonstration, the right hand holds the purple pen. In the

00:00:52.824 --> 00:00:55.670
current episode, the left hand approaches it.

00:00:56.100 --> 00:01:00.125
Look at the red cross. The pen is beside it in the current

00:01:00.125 --> 00:01:03.734
view. The cyan edges show where the finger pads will

00:01:03.734 --> 00:01:04.150
close.

00:01:04.580 --> 00:01:08.086
Later in the episode, the purple pen sits between the

00:01:08.086 --> 00:01:11.790
fingers. Both views use the same marks, even though they

00:01:11.790 --> 00:01:13.510
come from different hands.

00:01:13.940 --> 00:01:17.244
Each wrist image keeps the camera pose from its own

00:01:17.244 --> 00:01:20.936
observation. That lets the policy relate the image to the

00:01:20.936 --> 00:01:22.750
coordinates it uses to move.

00:01:23.008 --> 00:01:25.116
Watch the target approach the crosshair.

00:01:25.396 --> 00:01:26.016
Align.

00:01:26.308 --> 00:01:26.978
Close.

00:01:27.258 --> 00:01:27.766
Lift.

00:01:28.046 --> 00:01:32.533
Geometry makes the offset visible; the moving object reveals

00:01:32.533 --> 00:01:33.216
the result.

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:36.816
For an optional position estimate, the model picks the

00:01:36.816 --> 00:01:40.275
same feature in two views. Here it selected a corner of

00:01:40.275 --> 00:01:41.470
the pen holder rim.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:45.827
The rays meet at an angle of twenty seven degrees. Their

00:01:45.827 --> 00:01:49.755
closest separation is just over a tenth of a millimetre.

00:01:49.755 --> 00:01:52.630
That measures agreement between the rays.

00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:55.412
A target is only the beginning.

00:01:55.692 --> 00:01:57.812
The arm still needs a route it can execute.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.625
Here, the first preview fails inverse kinematics.

00:02:01.918 --> 00:02:03.100
No motion is sent.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.925
A revised plan passes.

00:02:05.205 --> 00:02:08.300
Now watch a translucent preview of the recorded arm motion:

00:02:08.605 --> 00:02:12.488
approach, grasp, lift, transfer, and pour.

00:02:12.768 --> 00:02:14.938
Joint targets change along the route.

00:02:15.218 --> 00:02:19.130
This preview explains the accepted sequence; it is not a second

00:02:19.130 --> 00:02:20.275
physical robot.

00:02:20.555 --> 00:02:24.612
The overlay clears, and the recorded arm completes the task.

00:02:24.900 --> 00:02:28.878
On 22 tasks shared with the original report,

00:02:28.878 --> 00:02:33.670
the category-balanced score rose from 52.27 to 80.58.

00:02:57.778 --> 00:03:00.898
MXI RoboICL.

00:03:01.191 --> 00:03:03.073
An MXI team upgrade.

00:03:03.378 --> 00:03:04.261
Thank you for watching.
